<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">townsfolk</ansi>

Towns remember. Some townsfolk are talkers: pass one idle in a settlement and
it may say what your company has done, to you by name, a little while after.
Each deed is told once. When there is nothing new to tell, a talker may
remark on the weather or the hour, or say nothing at all.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Reading it</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">townsfolk</ansi>           what has been said of you, and the deeds folk may yet speak of
  <ansi fg="command">renown</ansi>              the same

In the web client the Company window's Chronicle tab shows it too, above the
deeds: "What the towns say of you".

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What they speak of</ansi>

Talkers read your company's <ansi fg="command">chronicle</ansi> (<ansi fg="command">help chronicle</ansi>): bosses slain, relics
found, mercy and executions, the fallen, how scenes on the road ended. A deed
is worth mentioning for about two weeks of real time (some lines keep it
longer, up to 90 days), newest deed first. Which deeds a talker can tell
depends on the town: each world writes its own lines, so a deed with no line
for that talker goes unmentioned there.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What to know</ansi>

  - A deed is told once per player. What you have been told is kept through a
    restart and a copyover, and goes only when the character is deleted.
  - A talker speaks rarely: a few minutes between lines, and never the same
    line to you within the hour (the town's usual idle-chatter limits).
  - Some lines leave a <ansi fg="yellow-bold">mark</ansi> on the company, a flag that later lines (and some
    scenes, <ansi fg="command">help events</ansi>) can read: a town that has heard you slew its
    monster greets you differently afterwards. <ansi fg="command">townsfolk</ansi> lists the marks.
  - Nobody changes a price for what you did. Merchants buy and sell as ever.
  - Talk is real-time and costs nothing. It gives no gold, experience or power.

See also: <ansi fg="command">help chronicle</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help events</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help adventure</ansi>.
